WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.634
- Nahre how's it going?

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.736
- Hey LA.

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:04.004
- I got a question for you,

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:06.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
what is your
definition of music?

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.876
Languages evolve over time.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:12.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
If I wrote this message
and mailed it to the 1960s,

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:14.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
it will be dismissed
as nonsense.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:17.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like any language, what
is considered music

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.451
is also constantly changing.

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:24.624
When The Ramones released
their debut album in 1976,

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:27.727
parents hated it and radio
stations wouldn't play it.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.797
But three decades later, their
music was selling Diet Pepsi.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.201
There have always been
musicians and composers

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
that push the edges
of what we call music.

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:39.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
- In the early 20th
century some composers

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:43.176
began to reject conventional
ideas of harmony and tonality.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:46.946
And no composer did this more
boldly than Arnold Schoenberg

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:49.082 align:start size:88% position:13%
who wrote music that
was so against the times

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
that audiences would often
boo, laugh and heckle

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:53.753
at his performances.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.655
Imagine it's the 1920s,

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.191
your ears are probably more
accustomed to the sounds

00:00:58.191 --> 00:00:59.893
of composers such as Mozart.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then you hear performance
of Schoenberg's,

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.537
suit for piano Opus 25.

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:20.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
To most audiences it
sounded like random chaos

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:21.815
but what was Schoenberg doing?

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:25.318 align:start size:97% position:3%
Opus 25 was actually the
first piece composed entirely

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.520 line:0%
using the 12-tone technique,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.423 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
which is a compositional
approach that
Schoenberg invented.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.926
Using this technique,
you order the 12 tones

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.628
of the chromatic scale
in a particular order

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.630
called a tone row.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
So here's an example
of a tone row.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.705 align:start size:84% position:16%
The idea is to make
sure that all 12 tones

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.474
are equally emphasized.

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:49.342
This creates a sort of democracy

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:52.011
for tones don't relate
to a center like this.

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:59.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
The C clearly has more
weight over the other notes.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.157
A tone row doesn't have this.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.330
I ended on C but
the whole phrase

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.565
doesn't feel like
it's in C major.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.834
Schoenberg's rigid
ideas of a tonality

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.704
never caught on with
a widespread audience.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
But artists from other
musical backgrounds

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:22.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
would go on to explore
similar methods

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.110
later in the 20th century.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.912
Most notably in jazz.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.548
- In 1961, the Bill
Evans Trio recorded

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
and now legendary
live jazz album called

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.619
Sunday at the Village Vanguard.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.991
Do you hear those
traditional ideas

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:39.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
of harmony and
chord progressions?

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:42.061 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now check out this
excerpt from Free Jazz,

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.563
an album released that same year

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.365
by saxophonist Ornette Coleman.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:52.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is one of the first
albums to usher in free jazz.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.973
A movement that like Schoenberg

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
challenged traditional
ideas of tonality.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.211
The goal of artists like Coleman

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.613
wasn't necessarily to get
each tone equal emphasis

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.582
but rather to drop all the rules

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:05.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
so they can express themselves
as freely as possible.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.520
You can hear it in the works
of artists like Cecil Taylor,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.089
Albert Ayler and
even John Coltrane.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:16.996
Soon after free jazz
took off in the 1960s

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.531
three sisters from New Hampshire

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:21.167
also began making music that
challenged traditional ideas

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:24.003
of tonality and harmony
with a three piece band

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:27.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
called The Shaggs but unlike
Coltrane or Schoenberg

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.276 align:start position:0%
who were pushing music from
within established music scenes,

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:32.946 line:0%
The Shaggs we're definitely
pushing from the outside.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
- If someone thinks
The Shaggs are a bunch

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:45.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
of no talent hillbillies,
I understand that.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.827
Shaggs music doesn't reach out.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:50.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
The way that conventional
pop reaches out

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:54.067
and tries to, you know,
grab you with a hook.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.737
The Shaggs kind of exist
in a little bit of a bubble

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:00.440
and you've got to get
inside that bubble.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.744
And that I think applies
to the best outsider music.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.647 align:start size:88% position:13%
It pulls you into
something really unusual

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's one of the
adventures of outsider music

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.384
that I really love.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.853
If you don't like it, ignore it.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:16.089
Don't listen to me, it's okay.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.024
- One of the most famous
fans of outsider music

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:21.060
was David Bowie who based his
Ziggy Stardust as alter ego

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.929
in part on an outsider musician

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:25.098
named Legendary Stardust Cowboy.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
While the bulk of outsider
music never ventures

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.102
beyond open mics and
the record collections

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:32.105 align:start size:88% position:13%
of a few dedicated
fans, Legendary
Stardust Cowboy's music

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.375
has actually been played
in space, aboard Skylab

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:37.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
the American space
station launched in 1973.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:05.972 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Is that the bugle from
Legendary Stardust Cowboy?

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:13.179
Yes

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So what happens when a
composer wants to make a sound

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.790
that can't be created by
a traditional instrument?

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.224
You make your own.

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's what American
composer Harry Partch did.

00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:32.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
Partch was interested
in micro-tonality

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:35.702
and a 12 tone octave
just wasn't enough.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:39.205
He composed using 43 tones
within the traditional octave

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.341 align:start size:88% position:13%
and created instruments
out of everything

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:44.043 align:start size:88% position:13%
from old airplane
parts to liquor bottles

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.846 align:start size:84% position:16%
so that he could play
his compositions.

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:50.183
But no composer more boldly
challenged traditional ideas

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:54.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
of music and confused
audiences in the process

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.124 align:start position:0%
than John Cage an American whom
Arnold Schoenberg described

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:02.228
as not a composer but
an inventor of genius.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:03.963
Cage knew how to
play and compose

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:06.632 align:start size:97% position:3%
for traditional instruments
but he's most famous

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:09.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
for writing compositions that
featured sounds generated

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:11.371
from everyday objects.

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
He also pioneered the
idea of the prepared piano

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.017 line:0%
A piano that's been
temporarily altered

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:27.120 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
to produce exotic
and percussive sounds

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.456 line:0%
by placing objects like
screws, wood and rubber

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:31.557 line:0%
between the strings.

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.895 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Cage left many
decisions up to chance

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:38.030
to arrange his imaginary
landscape number four,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.800 align:start size:97% position:3%
which calls for 24
performers to turn 12 radios

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
to certain volumes and
stations at specific times.

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:46.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
Cage used an ancient
Chinese divination system

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:49.375 align:start size:97% position:3%
called the I-ching to decide
what should come next.

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.278 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Although cage was
making music with radios

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.647 align:start size:81% position:19%
and definitely
not for the radio,

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:57.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
his work has influenced
many well known musicians.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:00.353 line:0%
John Cale of The Velvet
Underground was a student

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:03.523 line:0%
of Cage and Radiohead's
Thom Yorke called him

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:06.159 line:0%
one of his all-time art heroes.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:09.929
Let's find a template that
is completely non musical

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:14.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then apply our
musical elements into it.

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:18.604
A sentence from the English
language or like a shape.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:21.507
- It has to be like a dodecagon.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.279
- OK let's use this
word Sound Field.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.079
The name Sound Field.

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:28.014
- Sound Field.

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:29.916
- Like S sounds like this.

00:07:29.916 --> 00:07:30.750
- Oh

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Why don't you take sound
and I will take field

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:35.521
- Field?

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.858
- Yeah and I won't share
how I'm interpreting it

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.394
until after and you do the
same 'cause we can do it.

00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:42.862
- Ok

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:45.698
- You can interpret it
because it looks this way

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:48.301
or it sounds this way, it
feels this way, whatever.

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:49.135
- That's gonna be tight

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:49.969
- Let's do it.

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:28.941
I'm more desensitized to
certain types of harmony

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:32.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
and certain types of
textures the older I get.

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:35.414 align:start size:88% position:13%
Once I'm exposed to
this type of dissonance,

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:37.083
let's talk about dissonance.

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.651
I get used to that.

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:43.089
And then the next time I hear
it it's no longer as jarring.

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.024
- Yeah it'll make you so curios.

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:48.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah kind of like food
you know, when you're a kid.

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:50.396
The older you get,

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:54.433 align:start position:0%
the more exposed you are to
different types of food I mean.

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:55.868
I remember when I was a kid,

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:58.437 align:start size:97% position:3%
I loved the smell of coffee
because it was sweet.

00:08:58.437 --> 00:09:02.008
But as soon as I tasted it I
thought, this isn't drinkable,

00:09:02.008 --> 00:09:03.276
this isn't edible.

00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:07.480
You acquire a taste for
it and then it changes.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:11.183
- That's probably how a
lot of fans of outsider

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:13.252 align:start size:97% position:3%
music became fans because
when you first hear it's like,

00:09:13.252 --> 00:09:14.754
What?

00:09:14.754 --> 00:09:17.356
I don't know anybody falls
in love at the first sound.

00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:21.360 line:0%
Where do you draw the line
between music and not music?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.763 line:0%
That is on the comments
and please subscribe.

